      "claim": "The absorption component is affirmatively supported by L4, while first-pass metabolism remains unquantified; therefore F >= 20% is better supported than F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C146", "C144", "C133", "C153"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "intestinal_absorption_permeability",
      "applicability": "high",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C146", "C144", "C133", "C153", "C120", "C137", "C143", "C161", "C147", "C168", "C131"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule or its exact structure.",
    "No matched oral versus intravenous exposure or absolute F value is available for the query.",
    "First-pass metabolism and systemic clearance of the query are not directly quantified.",
    "Solubility and permeability are inferred from the close analog trihexyphenidyl rather than measured on the query itself.",
    "The low neutral fraction (0.01) at physiological pH could still affect absorption, although the analog's high Caco-2 permeability suggests it is not limiting.",
    "No formulation or dose context is supplied for the query."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L4 adds high-permeability, GI-stability, and measurable solubility evidence for trihexyphenidyl, a close structural analog of the query, supporting the absorption component of oral bioavailability. No affirmative low-F evidence for the query appears. The high prediction is retained and strengthened with moderate confidence."
}

